1.1.m:
May the divine waters be for our help,
for our drinking;
may they flow upon us for peace and blessing.

1.2.m:
I invoke the divine waters,
where our cows drink;
an oblation is to be offered to the rivers.

Within the waters is immortality, within the waters is medicine;
and in the praises of the waters,
O horses, you become swift.

1.4.m:
We know your father, O reed,
Parjanya of abundant seed;
thereby I bring well-being to your body;
your pouring out is on the earth;
let it be outside with a splash!

I split open your urethra
like the dam of a reservoir;
thereby I bring well-being to your body;
your pouring out is on the earth;
let it be outside with a splash!

1.5.m:
Vaṣaṭ to you, O Pūṣan! For her let Aryaman,
the priest, the wise, make a proper clearance;
let the woman loosen herself,
let the joints separate, so that she may give birth!

The four quarters and the four
intermediate directions of the earth;
the gods impelled the embryo;
let them open [her] up to give birth!

Let the well-formed one open up;
we cause the womb to part.
Loosen, O Sūṣaṇā, you;
release down, O Viṣkalā!

Neither in the flesh, nor in the fat,
nor fixed in anything whatsoever;
let the afterbirth fall down!

1.6.m:
Invoked is the Lord of Speech;
invoked am I by the Lord of Speech.
May I be united with what is heard [sacred knowledge];
may I not be separated from what is heard.

1.9.m:
Homage be to your wrath, O King Varuṇa;
for, O god, you perceive every deceit.
I send forth a hundred, a thousand others;
may this man of ours live through [his] autumns.

1.10.m:
Varuṇa speaks in favor of lead;
Agni assists lead;
Indra gave me lead;
let it be the driver-away of disease.

This overcomes the Viṣkandha-curse;
this beats back the voracious demons;
with this I will conquer all
that are offspring of the Piśācī.

1.11.m:
[Overcome] those who are our adversaries;
tread upon him who attacks [us];
[overcome] him who plots malice against us.

1.12.m:
To old age alone may this man grow;
let not other deaths [take] him from you;
as a caring mother her son in her lap,
may Mitra protect him from distress, in friendship.

1.13.m:
May abundance accompany the drinker of it;
may this strength and ancestral food accompany him.
Indra first poured forth this [potion] when pierced;
strength and ancestral food, unaging, is this of yours.
By it live for [many] autumns, full of lustre;
let it not leak out for you; the physicians have made [it] for you.

1.15.m:
This girl of yours, O king,
a bride, is shaken down;
let her be bound in her mother's house,
then in her brother's, then in her father's.

1.21.m:
Not bearing offspring, blackish,
the Apacit-gland will fly away;
the boil will fly away from here;
trickling away, it will perish.

1.22.m:
May there be wellbeing for our mother and father,
wellbeing for our cattle and for our people;
may everything be well-being and benevolent for us;
so may we behold the sun.

1.27.m:
May Soma and Savitṛ make freedom from fear;
freedom from fear may heaven and earth both make;
may there be freedom from fear for our light and intermediate space;
with the oblation of the Seven Rṣis may there be freedom from fear for us.

May heaven and earth be freedom from fear for us here;
with Agni burn back our enemies facing us.
May they find neither an ally nor a support;
slaying one another, let them go to death.

1.31.m:
Highest is your name,
highest is your father's name;
from where you are born, O Kuṣṭha,
come from there for safety.

1.33.m:
Today I have gone after the waters;
we have united with their sap.
Rich in milk, O Agni, I have come;
unite me with splendor.

Unite me, O Agni, with splendor,
and make me abundant in offspring;
may the gods know of this [deed] of mine;
may Indra know together with the ṛṣis.

Look upon me, O waters, with an auspicious eye;
with an auspicious body touch my skin.
The auspicious fires dwelling in the waters we invoke;
confer lordly power and splendor upon me, O goddesses.

1.37.m:
Both kinds having arrived, I have turned away from you;
some have been shot by the gods,
and many others to heaven.

Homage to your [arrow], O Rudra, as it is shot;
homage to the [arrows] fitted [to the bowstring];
homage to the [arrows] being discharged;
homage to the [arrows] fallen down!

My coverings
are six thousands and six hundreds;
with them we enclose ourselves;
may they protect us on all sides.
Much other is distributed here;
pierce through its desire!

An iron shelter has been made great for me
out of fear of you,
to be revered with reverence;
with it we enclose ourselves;
may that protect us on all sides.
[…]

I make an iron armor,
I make an iron door,
I make iron pegs;
may they protect us on all sides.
Much other is distributed here;
pierce through its desire!

1.39.m:
O Agni, come to us with cows;
O Indu, accompany us with wealth;
Indra is the supporter in our homes.

Savitar, who possesses thousands,
may he rejoice in our homes;
may nourishment come, may goods come!

May Dhātar grant us wealth,
he who is ruler, lord of the world;
may he bestow upon us with fullness.

1.40.m:
Mine are cows, mine are horses,
mine are goats and sheep;
mine alone have men become;
mine is all this that possesses a soul,
moving and breathing is under my control.

1.41.m:
Turn back again with vigor,
again, O Agni, with full life-span;
protect us again from distress.

1.42.m:
With jaws [crush] the marauders, O Agni,
and with two tusks the thieves;
with two jaws the robbers, O bounteous one;
chew them well chewed!

1.43.m:
O runner around, run around;
O caller back, bring [him] back;
and let Indra and Agni
bring that person here to me.

1.46.m:
May the gift protect the pierced one from thirst;
may the gift [protect him] from hunger, the wise giver [be protected] from death.
May he who gives become free from the Viṣkandha-curse;
he who is bountiful swells with the sacrificial fee.

Let the bountiful one swell with the sacrificial fee;
guard [him] all around like sewn armor.
Let the poison be outside his body;
let the arrow-head fall far away from him!

1.51.m:
May Agni straightway be our guide;
may Bṛhaspati be our companion in gain.
I call upon Indra, slayer of Vṛtra, destroyer of forts;
with fortune may we today be fortunate!

1.53.m:
O renowned Agni, increase this man with renown;
bring here the homage that belongs to Indra.
May he be the head, Parameṣṭhin, splendid;
may he be of highest fame among his peers!

Seeking what is auspicious, the heaven-finding seers
sat down in austerity and consecration at the beginning;
thence kingdom, strength, and might were born;
let the gods bend that down to him!

Turn toward [him], all of you, together toward us;
let this watcher be your overlord.
Confer together with a common counsel;
hereafter may all of you live depending on him!

2.21.m:
O Agni, let this woman find a husband;
for let King Soma make her fortunate!
Bearing sons, she will be chief queen;
having gone to her husband, fortunate, may she rule widely!

2.22.m:
Happily may the men [plow] with plow and oxen,
Bhaga with plowshares, the Lord of the Field with the Maruts;
let Parjanya urge the seed, let him stimulate this;
O Śunāsīra, make grain here!

2.23.m:
The Rudras in the unexhausted spring constantly
rejoice—the gods, the ancestors, humans;
this is the portion, and the lord of the portion, O gods;
may we cross the wide, unconquerable autumns!

2.27.m:
With two horns it thrusts away the rakṣas;
with the root, the sorceresses;
with the middle, it drives off consumption;
evil does not overstep it […].

It has produced a hundred heroes;
it has cast off a hundred consumptions;
having slain all the evil-named [demons],
[…] it shakes off the rakṣases.

2.31.m:
Let the cutting one go away,
as if tearing up the avakā-weed.
Let the [guards] go in all directions,
in all directions the highwaymen;
in all directions, through the recurring plant, [their] mind;
unsuccessful are the malicious!

2.32.m:
The liquorice that is born from the north,
from the liquorice-plant,
I know the remedy in it;
my tongue is full of honey.

The embryo of the plants are you,
and you were the embryo of the waters;
also you are Soma's brother;
O liquorice, favor my word!

2.33.m:
Let your hairs stand erect,
let your eyes dry up with yearning;
let [the cow] unite with the calf,
as joy [mixes] with surā!

2.40.m:
Hold fast to the dear ascent;
may the home of floods delight here!
By this Prajāpati released the swift currents;
hold fast to the shelter here, O Sarasvatīs!

2.41.m:
My sons are slayers of enemies,
and my daughter is a sovereign queen;
and I am victorious;
upon my husband my fame is supreme!
